WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.749
After you understand individual packets, Wireshark can help you group related traffic.

2
00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:11.421
Conversations show communication pairs, such as two IP addresses or two TCP endpoints.

3
00:00:11.421 --> 00:00:21.998
This is useful when a capture has many packets and you need to see which systems talked the most, which ports were involved, or which pair deserves deeper inspection.

4
00:00:21.998 --> 00:00:25.907
Streams are another way to follow related packet sequences.

5
00:00:25.907 --> 00:00:34.952
Following a TCP stream can present the application conversation in order when that content is visible and when you are authorized to view it.

6
00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:43.689
This can be helpful in an approved lab or troubleshooting capture, but it must be handled carefully. Streams may reveal sensitive data.

7
00:00:43.689 --> 00:00:52.581
If private data, credentials, tokens, cookies, or personal information appear, protect the capture and report only what policy allows.

8
00:00:52.581 --> 00:01:02.162
Wireshark statistics provide summary views. Protocol hierarchy shows the mix of protocols in the capture. Endpoints show communicating systems.

9
00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:08.140
Conversations show pairs of systems and ports. I/O graphs can show traffic volume over time.

10
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:15.422
Expert information can point out conditions Wireshark noticed, such as retransmissions or malformed packets.

11
00:01:15.422 --> 00:01:23.776
These views are investigation guides, not final proof. A large number of packets to one endpoint may be normal for a file transfer.

12
00:01:23.776 --> 00:01:28.375
A warning may be caused by capture loss rather than a real network fault.

13
00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:36.730
A protocol count may reflect where you captured, not the whole environment. Use summaries to decide where to inspect more deeply.

14
00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:48.151
A practical pattern is to start with a question, look at a statistic that might narrow the field, apply a display filter, inspect packets, and then return to the summary if needed.

15
00:01:48.151 --> 00:01:55.126
This back-and-forth keeps the work grounded. You are not trying to make the statistics tell the whole story.

16
00:01:55.126 --> 00:02:00.108
You are using them to find the packets that can support a careful explanation.
